WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.339
I've been hearing about Hillsdale my
entire life. When I visited, I fell in

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:06.180
love with the place.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:09.750
It's intangible, but there's just
something about Hillsdale that really was

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:14.190
attractive to me. I had the opportunity
really to interact with a lot of

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:19.410
different people. I have my Chi Omega
sisters. I got involved in the Collegian.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:23.550
As I've been thinking about it, I come
back to the time I spent writing for the

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:29.340
Collegian. It was just a really fun
experience. My first real class where I had

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:33.270
the opportunity to study U.S. Supreme
Court cases was a constitutional law

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:39.030
class at Hillsdale. It was the first time I
really read the cases and thought about

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:44.219
the impact in a negative way that
judicial activism can have-learning the

00:00:44.219 --> 00:00:49.890
history of the Court's decisions and how
it really changed the country during the

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:54.510
times when the court was dominated by
folks who didn't respect the rule of law.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:01:00.030
It made a huge impact on me in terms of
looking ahead to be a lawyer. I had run

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:05.460
for Supreme Court in 2008. I became
interested in running for Supreme Court

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:09.840
again when the legislature changed it to
a nonpartisan race, and so I got

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.400
interested again and decided to run. I ran
against four opponents in a five-way

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.100
race, ended up winning forty percent of
the vote.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.759
I'm looking forward to being a leader on
the court. My approach is to follow the

00:01:20.759 --> 00:01:25.170
rule of law, rather than decide how a
case ought to turn out and reason

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:29.790
backwards from there. When I learned I
was receiving this award, I was surprised.

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:35.100
My parents, I think, both won the award,
but I never really considered myself a

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.159
candidate for it, so I was very
pleasantly surprised.

00:01:38.159 --> 00:01:43.439
Hillsdale College instilled in me a real
commitment, I think, to service,

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:49.460
whether it be public service or service
to your family or to an institution or

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:53.990
where you're going to go to work. I've been
lucky to watch the college grow and

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:58.369
change since I graduated in 1987 to now, and the transformation

00:01:58.369 --> 00:02:03.740
has been incredibly positive, incredibly
inspiring. I'm really proud to be a

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:04.920
Hillsdale grad.

